In article <1584@ucl-cs.uucp> J.Purchase@cs.ucl.ac.uk (Jan Purchase) writes:

>exist endless ways to progress (or die!). However, there are occasions when
>this reliance on random numbers become painfully obvious. After eating a
>floating eye and blinding oneself, one can see monsters just appearing on a
>level at random places - do all monsters have a teleport instrinsic? Why do
>they just appear in places where there are no staircases, trapdoors or pits?
>Are they all wondering on to level teleport traps? Sounds a bit fishy to me.

In fact, you don't have to have telepathy to see that.  Have you never
seen a monster randomly generated in the same room as your character?

Random monster generation, in my opinion, is a great enhancement to
the game, and easily overrides realism, which I consider but a
tangental concern.  But if you *must* have a logical explanation for
it, just think of it as the Wiz sending out monsters to keep you from
getting to him.

>A second, unrelated point is that the user interface of NetHack needs
>improvement. It is bad HCI practice and very frustrating for users when they
>are bombarded by endless messages which end in --More-- and prevented from
>responding in any other way than pressing the space bar.
>
>For example:
>
>the nymph pretends to be friendly --More-- 
>the nymph removes your blessed +9 blue dragon scale mail --More-- 
>the ancient red Dragon bites --More-- 
>the nymph removes your blessed +6 blue and white shield --More-- 
>the ancient red Dragon breathes on you! You are on fire! --More-- 
>the mumakil hits! the mumakil bites! the mumakil hits --More-- 
>the nymph removes your +8 hard hat --More-- 
>the mumakil hits! the mumakil hits! the mumakil hits --More-- 
>the ancient red Dragon bites --More-- 
>the nymph removes your +6 hard shoes --More-- 
>the ancient red Dragon breathes on you! You are on fire! --More--
>you die...--More-- 

Just a nit pick--nymphs don't hang around stealing things.  They steal
for one round and teleport away.  Anyway, to the point:

>This type of interaction is highly frustrating. Realistic, yes ok maybe the
>character deserved to die (like he bumped into a floating eye or
>something), but people play games to have fun not to have
>their characters mindlessly killed without chance of reply. I know, I have
>been a DM for 8 years.  A modicum of frustration is to be expected, indeed,
>it makes a game addictive, but instant death situations in which the user is
>given no chance to even respond are no fun. A basic principle of HCI is give
>the user the illusion of control at all times.

I couldn't disagree more.  Yes, it's frustrating, but what's the
alternative?  Are you saying that sleep, paralysis, and freezing in
place should all be done away with?   Or are you saying that if you
die while immobile you should just be told that you die, and not how
you died?  I would find that a lot more irritating than watching as
you're helplessly hacked to pieces.

You are frozen by the gelatinous cube!  --more--
You die... --more--

And what if you don't die while immobile?  Are you suggesting that
while you are immobile, the game should store everything that happens
in a buffer, and only tells you what happened if you survive?  That's
ridiculous from a mechanical point of view, and my personal opinion is
that it would detract from, and not improve, the game.

>I maintain a UNIX system on which half the users are regular NetHack
>players. Some time ago one of them was showing off his 12th level wizard to
>me. The best character he had ever played. He was merrily turning all the
>creatures he fought to stone using a cockatrice corpse. He was careful not
>to venture down the stairs with the corpse to avoid falling on it and he was
>keeping two lizards in reserve in case he fell victim to petrification. While
>resting in a room (being careful not to typeahead spaces), an incubus
>appeared right next to him (see point above) and took off his gloves! He was
>instantly turned to stone -- all in one round! The player was pissed off in
>the extreme and I was stunned. 
>
>a) Can an incubus gate itself in, or otherwise teleport to a locale?

Nope.  This goes back to your original complaint--random monster
generation.

>b) How can you take off someone's gloves (or charm them to do so) while they
>still hold a weapon, especially a corpse!

I agree.  That's one thing I think is stupid, and should be fixed.
Perhaps the incubus should turn to stone trying to take the cockatrice
out of your hands?

A similar thing happens if your gloves are removed (by an incubus,
succubus, or nymph) when you're weilding a cursed weapon--you can't
put them back on.  Either the cursed weapon should prevent the gloves
from being removed, or it should be removed along with the gloves (the
latter would probably be more consistent).

>c) why is the touch of a corkatrice instantly petrifying when it is dead, but
>takes 2-3 moves while it lives. Is it logical for it to be more potent in
>death than in life?

Because there is a difference between the cockatrice touching you for
a moment and you grasping it in your hands.  Although I agree that it
should take a few moves to turn to stone from a cockatrice in either
case, because cockatrices are responsible for some of the cheapest
ways to die in Nethack.  But you shouldn't get a saving throw if you
actually hold the cockatrice. 

>The player has not played the game since, preferring, in his words, to
>indulge in games of skill rather than games of chance. I think this is
>somewhat extreme, but I understand his reasoning. Is NetHack a game of
>skill? If so, will NetHack 3.1 have less of a dependency on random numbers
>and a better user interface?

While I disagree which what you're referring to as a "better
interface", I can see why that person you're talking about is pissed,
and hope something is done about cockatrices.

>Would anyone out there care to post interesting stories of the unfair deaths
>of their characters?

Yes, falling into a pit while wielding a cockatrice.

